### What are the core entities?

The core entities are `Logger`, `LogLevel`, `Appender`, `Formatter`, and `LoggerFactory`.

```java
package com.bank.logging;

public enum LogLevel {
    DEBUG(0), INFO(1), WARN(2), ERROR(3);

    private final int severity;

    LogLevel(int severity) {
        this.severity = severity;
    }

    public boolean isEnabled(LogLevel threshold) {
        return this.severity >= threshold.severity;
    }
}
```

### How does the Logger work?

The Logger holds a minimum threshold and a list of appenders. Messages below the threshold are discarded cheaply.

```java
package com.bank.logging;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

public class Logger {
    private final String name;
    private LogLevel threshold;
    private final List<Appender> appenders;
    private Formatter formatter;

    public Logger(String name, LogLevel threshold,
                  Formatter formatter) {
        this.name = name;
        this.threshold = threshold;
        this.appenders = new ArrayList<>();
        this.formatter = formatter;
    }

    public void addAppender(Appender appender) {
        appenders.add(appender);
    }

    public void log(LogLevel level, Supplier<String> messageSupplier) {
        if (!level.isEnabled(threshold)) return;
        String formatted = formatter.format(
                level, name, messageSupplier.get());
        for (Appender appender : appenders) {
            appender.append(formatted);
        }
    }

    public void debug(Supplier<String> msg) {
        log(LogLevel.DEBUG, msg);
    }

    public void info(Supplier<String> msg) {
        log(LogLevel.INFO, msg);
    }

    public void warn(Supplier<String> msg) {
        log(LogLevel.WARN, msg);
    }

    public void error(Supplier<String> msg) {
        log(LogLevel.ERROR, msg);
    }
}
```

### Why use supplier lambdas for messages?

Passing messages as a `Supplier<String>` defers string building until the level is confirmed enabled. In a hot transfer path at National Bank of India, building log strings that get thrown away is pure waste.

```java
// Efficient: string only built if INFO is enabled
logger.info(() -> "Transfer " + amount + " from " + from
        + " to " + to);

// Wasteful: string always built, even if disabled
logger.info("Transfer " + amount + " from " + from
        + " to " + to);
```

### How does the Appender interface work?

The Appender is a Strategy for where logs go. Adding a new destination is a new class.

```java
package com.bank.logging;

public interface Appender {
    void append(String formattedMessage);
}
```

```java
package com.bank.logging;

public class ConsoleAppender implements Appender {
    @Override
    public synchronized void append(String formattedMessage) {
        System.out.println(formattedMessage);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.logging;

import java.io.*;

public class FileAppender implements Appender {
    private final PrintWriter writer;

    public FileAppender(String filePath) throws IOException {
        this.writer = new PrintWriter(
                new FileWriter(filePath, true));
    }

    @Override
    public synchronized void append(String formattedMessage) {
        writer.println(formattedMessage);
        writer.flush();
    }
}
```

### How does the Formatter work?

The Formatter turns level, timestamp, logger name, and message into a formatted line. It is pluggable independently of the appender.

```java
package com.bank.logging;

import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;

public class SimpleFormatter implements Formatter {
    private static final DateTimeFormatter TS =
            DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss");

    @Override
    public String format(LogLevel level, String loggerName,
                          String message) {
        return LocalDateTime.now().format(TS)
                + " [" + level + "] "
                + loggerName + " - " + message;
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.logging;

public interface Formatter {
    String format(LogLevel level, String loggerName, String message);
}
```

